---
title: Use files
description: Read, write, copy, list, and remove files inside a sandbox.
---

`filesystem` is on the sandbox handle. Daytona and Modal both implement it. Lifecycle calls stay on the handle.

A path inside the sandbox is absolute, such as `/tmp/kit-files/note.txt`. A relative path is rejected before the provider is called. A local path for copy may be relative to your process.

`readFile` and `writeFile` use bytes. Encode text in your own code. `copyFromLocal` and `copyToLocal` use paths on your disk.

`makeDirectory` creates missing parent directories. Modal accepts a directory that already exists. Daytona returns an error when it already exists.

`remove` deletes a file, or an empty directory. Set `recursive: true` to delete a directory and its contents.

`stat` on a symlink describes the symlink. Modal can report a symlink. Daytona reports a file or a directory. `size` is a byte count (`bigint` in TypeScript). `mode` is an octal string when the provider supplies one.

An omitted timeout uses the client deadline. `timeout: 0` adds no Kit deadline. A Daytona timeout must be a whole number of seconds. Modal uses the abort signal as the deadline.

The snippets below assume you already have a `sandbox` handle. Build one on the provider page, then delete or terminate it when you are finished.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

